name: functions
intro: A function is a named piece of code with its own variables. You have
intro: been writing one all along: `main`. Splitting a program into small
intro: functions is how C stays readable.
intro:
intro:     static int square(int x)
intro:     {
intro:         return x * x;
intro:     }
intro:
intro: `int` before the name is the return type; `(int x)` is the parameter.
intro: `static` means the name is private to this file -- use it on every
intro: function that nothing else calls. It stops name clashes and lets the
intro: compiler warn you about code nobody uses.
intro:
intro: C reads top to bottom, so a function must be known before it is used.
intro: Either define it above its caller, or put a prototype -- the first
intro: line with a `;` -- near the top and define it later:
intro:
intro:     static int square(int x);
intro:
intro:     int main(void)
intro:     {
intro:         printf("%d\n", square(7));
intro:         return 0;
intro:     }
intro:
intro:     static int square(int x)
intro:     {
intro:         return x * x;
intro:     }
intro:
intro: Arguments are passed by value: the function gets a copy. Changing `x`
intro: inside `square` never touches the caller's variable. (To change the
intro: caller's data you need pointers -- that is a later unit.)
intro:
intro: A function may call itself. Every recursion needs a base case that does
intro: not recurse, or it never ends and the stack runs out:
intro:
intro:     static unsigned sum_to(unsigned n)
intro:     {
intro:         if (n == 0)
intro:             return 0;
intro:         return n + sum_to(n - 1);
intro:     }
intro:
intro: `main` returns int because that number is the exit status the caller
intro: reads. A function that promises a value must return one on every path.
---
q: fact.c -- factorials by recursion
spec: Write a `static uint64_t factorial(unsigned n)` that calls itself, with
spec: a prototype above `main`. Print the factorials of 0 to 10, one per line,
spec: like `5! = 120`. `0!` is 1.
file: fact.c
prog: #include <inttypes.h>
prog: #include <stdint.h>
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: 
prog: static uint64_t factorial(unsigned n);
prog: 
prog: int main(void)
prog: {
prog: 	for (unsigned n = 0; n <= 10; n++)
prog: 		printf("%u! = %" PRIu64 "\n", n, factorial(n));
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
prog: 
prog: static uint64_t factorial(unsigned n)
prog: {
prog: 	if (n <= 1)
prog: 		return 1;
prog: 	return n * factorial(n - 1);
prog: }
case: fact
case: fact | head -n 1
case: fact | tail -n 1
hint: the base case must cover 0 as well as 1, or factorial(0) never stops
